            <ArticleTitle>Effect of magnesium sulfate on morphine activity retention to control pain after herniorrhaphy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>119-125</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/pmt-2016-0038</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This research was carried out to compare magnesium sulfate (MgSO4) with isotonic saline in terms of pain control after herniorrhaphy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS &amp; METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A randomized double-blind study, in which the patients were blind to all. A total of 100 patients who were candidates of herniorrhaphy were randomized into two groups: experimental and control (50 patients in each). Anesthesia was induced with 20% of 4 cc of morphine. The experimental and control group received postoperative 20% of 2 cc MgSO4 in 2 cc of isotonic saline and 4 cc of isotonic saline, respectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The administration of postoperative morphine in control group 0.79 ± 1.48 mg was significantly higher to the experimental group 0.17 ± 0.63 mg during the first 24 h (p = 0.01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">MgSO4 increased the potency of morphine thereby reducing the amount of postoperative pain killer needed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
